WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.491
The guide compares the five providers,

2
00:00:02.491 --> 00:00:06.075
and one row matters most here:
who says action is required.

3
00:00:06.075 --> 00:00:09.789
Azure's Service Health, read
through Azure Resource Graph,

4
00:00:09.789 --> 00:00:12.187
has no field the kit reads that says so.

5
00:00:12.187 --> 00:00:16.967
So the kit applies a stated rule:
a retirement is action required,

6
00:00:16.967 --> 00:00:20.934
due at the advisory's end
time, and a security advisory

7
00:00:20.934 --> 00:00:24.901
is action required with no
date, for the ISSO to review.

8
00:00:24.901 --> 00:00:28.962
Microsoft 365 has its act-by date field.

9
00:00:28.962 --> 00:00:31.724
Oracle Cloud Infrastructure has its action

10
00:00:31.724 --> 00:00:34.337
required type and action required by date.

11
00:00:34.337 --> 00:00:37.187
AWS has its actionability field.

12
00:00:37.187 --> 00:00:40.884
And Google Cloud's Personalized
Service Health doesn't say,

13
00:00:40.884 --> 00:00:45.863
and has no planned maintenance at all: its
event category has one value, incident.

14
00:00:45.863 --> 00:00:47.967
Wherever the provider doesn't say,

15
00:00:47.967 --> 00:00:51.800
the record's action basis begins
with rule, and states the rule.

16
00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:55.300
Nobody mistakes the kit's inference
for the provider's own word.
